1
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:08,000
OK, let's get started

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
As a recap of some of the stuff that we've talked about so far

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,440
we've formulated this reinforcement learning problem

4
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:16,840
where we have states and actions

5
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:20,600
We have some trajectories that we might be collecting

6
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:22,780
which are sequences of states and actions

7
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:24,360
We have a reward function, which tells

8
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:29,240
us how good is a particular state and action

9
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:31,080
and also a policy, which is usually

10
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:32,960
what we're trying to learn

11
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:35,800
And this is representing the behavior

12
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,000
that we're trying to learn

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,060
And then the goal of reinforcement learning

14
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:42,420
which we talked about in the first lecture

15
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:46,700
is to try to maximize the expected sum of rewards

16
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:53,880
So we want to produce behavior that leads to positive outcomes

17
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:57,200
And we can actually mathematically write this

18
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:00,480
down as an expectation over the probability distribution

19
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:03,600
over trajectories, where this probability distribution is

20
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:06,480
a function of the policy which is acting

21
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:09,560
in the world, the environment dynamics which

22
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:17,240
might be stochastic, and of course, the reward function

23
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,000
Last week on Friday, we started talking about one way

24
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,420
to do this, which is if you have expert demonstrations

25
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:24,840
you could try to imitate the expert

26
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:29,920
by trying to maximize the likelihood of the actions

27
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:32,600
that the demonstrator did-- so trying to actually produce

28
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:36,520
a policy that leads to actions that the expert would

29
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:37,380
have taken

30
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:39,680
This is a very simple and scalable approach

31
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:43,000
but it cannot outperform the demonstrator and doesn't allow

32
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,720
improvement through trial and error

33
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:50,080
We also introduced the notion of an offline algorithm that

34
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:53,280
only uses an existing data set, and no new data

35
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:56,120
from a learned policy, and an online algorithm that

36
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:59,230
uses new data from the learned policy

37
00:01:59,230 --> 00:02:01,750
And today we'll be talking about ways

38
00:02:01,750 --> 00:02:03,200
in which we can use reinforcement

39
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:04,830
learning to outperform the demonstrator

40
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:06,290
to improve from practice

41
00:02:06,290 --> 00:02:07,750
And specifically, we'll be starting

42
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:10,310
to talk about online reinforcement learning

43
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:13,150
algorithms

44
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:15,110
Particularly, our first online RL algorithm

45
00:02:15,110 --> 00:02:17,070
will be policy gradients

46
00:02:17,070 --> 00:02:19,470
We'll talk about a couple of different variants of policy

47
00:02:19,470 --> 00:02:23,570
gradients and introduce both the mathematical derivation for it

48
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:26,150
as well as some of the intuition behind it

49
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:28,350
And policy gradients are the basis

50
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:31,310
for some popular reinforcement learning algorithms, including

51
00:02:31,310 --> 00:02:35,270
ones that are used to train legged robots, algorithms that

52
00:02:35,270 --> 00:02:37,870
are used to train language models

53
00:02:37,870 --> 00:02:41,590
and are also part of the default project

54
00:02:41,590 --> 00:02:43,270
We won't get the complete algorithm

55
00:02:43,270 --> 00:02:47,990
that was used to train these today, but we'll get most of--

56
00:02:47,990 --> 00:02:49,650
or halfway there, basically

57
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:54,270
And by Friday, we'll have most of the algorithm for it

58
00:02:54,270 --> 00:02:55,830
And then the key goals will be to try

59
00:02:55,830 --> 00:02:57,930
to get the intuition behind policy gradients

60
00:02:57,930 --> 00:03:03,510
how to implement them, and when to use them

61
00:03:03,510 --> 00:03:04,430
OK

62
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:08,510
So the outline of an online reinforcement learning algorithm

63
00:03:08,510 --> 00:03:11,010
is going to look something like this

64
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:15,690
So first, we need to initialize our policy in some way

65
00:03:15,690 --> 00:03:17,190
When you train a neural network, you

66
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:18,950
need to initialize its weights

67
00:03:18,950 --> 00:03:21,880
We could initialize the weights of our policy randomly

68
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:24,050
We could also initialize it with imitation learning

69
00:03:24,050 --> 00:03:26,630
We could also use heuristics

70
00:03:26,630 --> 00:03:29,270
It depends on the problem setting

71
00:03:29,270 --> 00:03:31,550
And after we initialize our policy

72
00:03:31,550 --> 00:03:34,030
we'll run our policy to collect some data

73
00:03:34,030 --> 00:03:38,190
So actually, try out different things in the world

74
00:03:38,190 --> 00:03:42,670
Then we'll use that batch of data to improve the policy

75
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:44,250
And then we will repeat that process

76
00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:47,270
We'll then collect new data with our updated policy

77
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:51,710
and update our policy again using that latest batch of data

78
00:03:51,710 --> 00:03:54,650
So we're going to attempt the task and try to get better

79
00:03:54,650 --> 00:03:56,860
So what this might look like is the first iteration

80
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:59,980
we attempt whatever--

81
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:02,980
some kind of simple 2D navigation task

82
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:04,960
The reward for some of them is bad

83
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:08,060
The reward for other trajectories is OK

84
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:10,900
And then after more iterations of this

85
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:12,860
we might have a policy that's a bit better that

86
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:15,420
is able to get a higher reward but isn't perfect

87
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:16,959
So sometimes it gets a higher reward

88
00:04:16,959 --> 00:04:20,260
sometimes it still gets a low reward

89
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,500
Then we'll use that to improve the policy again

90
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:25,180
And after more iterations, hopefully, we

91
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:30,020
will get a policy that is able to succeed

92
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:32,780
So this is this notion of online reinforcement learning

93
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:35,260
where we're collecting data online and improving

94
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:38,320
using that data

95
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:39,860
So we talked about this reinforcement

96
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:41,220
learning objective

97
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:45,020
And say you have some policy, and you

98
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:48,510
want to actually understand how good that policy is

99
00:04:48,510 --> 00:04:50,140
to do that, we can't just actually look

100
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:51,680
at the weights of the neural network

101
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:53,380
We actually have to run the policy

102
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:55,380
to understand how good it is

103
00:04:55,380 --> 00:05:01,180
And so what we'll do is to estimate this expectation, which

104
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:03,200
is a function of the policy

105
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:05,780
We can actually just run the policy multiple times

106
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:11,580
observe the resulting reward, and then compute the average

107
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:13,060
over the observed reward

108
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:19,780
And that's an estimate of how good our policy is

109
00:05:19,780 --> 00:05:24,140
So we can then sum over the samples from our policy

110
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:26,910
Now, let's start to think about how we actually-- so we have

111
00:05:26,910 --> 00:05:28,120
this trial and error process

112
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:31,860
We collect some data and we improve our policy

113
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:33,680
Collecting the data is straightforward

114
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:35,740
We've talked about how to run a policy

115
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:37,680
to get a rollout in the environment

116
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:40,320
We can sample an action, observe the next state

117
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:43,540
then run that through our policy again to get a new action

118
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:45,300
and repeat that process

119
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:48,220
But how do we actually then improve our policy

120
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:51,220
after we've collected that data

121
00:05:51,220 --> 00:05:56,050
So we have our objective, and this can be written down--

122
00:05:56,050 --> 00:05:58,010
we have some policy pi-theta

123
00:05:58,010 --> 00:06:04,370
And our goal is to maximize the expected rewards when

124
00:06:04,370 --> 00:06:06,110
we sample from that policy

125
00:06:06,110 --> 00:06:09,370
So we can consider the trajectories that are sampled

126
00:06:09,370 --> 00:06:14,250
from the policy as being drawn by some distribution that

127
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:16,890
I'll write as p-theta of tau--

128
00:06:16,890 --> 00:06:19,490
theta because the distribution over trajectories

129
00:06:19,490 --> 00:06:24,290
is a function of the policy that you're using

130
00:06:24,290 --> 00:06:28,810
And our goal is to maximize the sum of rewards

131
00:06:28,810 --> 00:06:30,650
I'm going to use shorthand here

132
00:06:30,650 --> 00:06:36,890
And I'm going to use shorthand for r of the trajectory, where

133
00:06:36,890 --> 00:06:43,530
this just means the sum of the rewards along that trajectory

134
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:47,290
So our goal is to maximize the rewards

135
00:06:47,290 --> 00:06:49,210
We're going to maximize this with respect

136
00:06:49,210 --> 00:06:53,490
to our policy parameters theta

137
00:06:53,490 --> 00:06:57,150
Now, how do we actually optimize this

138
00:06:57,150 --> 00:06:59,450
So we want to use gradient descent because we typically

139
00:06:59,450 --> 00:07:01,280
train neural networks with gradient descent

140
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:03,810
We need to figure out the gradient of this objective

141
00:07:03,810 --> 00:07:06,610
with regard to theta

142
00:07:06,610 --> 00:07:15,970
And we'll refer to this term right here as j-theta

143
00:07:15,970 --> 00:07:17,470
to be able to write it

144
00:07:17,470 --> 00:07:20,680
This is the objective that we're trying to maximize

145
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:22,050
And it's a little bit challenging

146
00:07:22,050 --> 00:07:25,410
to try to get a gradient of this thing

147
00:07:25,410 --> 00:07:28,170
directly because the relationship

148
00:07:28,170 --> 00:07:30,650
that this has on theta is through the trajectories

149
00:07:30,650 --> 00:07:32,210
that we sampled

150
00:07:32,210 --> 00:07:36,850
So we can't just differentiate through the sampling process

151
00:07:36,850 --> 00:07:38,650
And that sampling process also depends

152
00:07:38,650 --> 00:07:42,940
on the dynamics of the world, which we also don't know

153
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:44,570
So if we're going to think about trying

154
00:07:44,570 --> 00:07:53,040
to get a gradient of j-theta, it's

155
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:59,560
helpful to write down this expectation as an integral

156
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:03,880
So this expectation can be written

157
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:14,520
as an integral of p-theta of tau times r of tau d tau

158
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:20,440
So this is just from the definition of an expectation

159
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:23,790
Now, because an integral is a summation

160
00:08:23,790 --> 00:08:25,160
and that's a linear operation, we

161
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:30,200
can bring the gradient inside the integral

162
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:32,919
And so in particular, what that looks

163
00:08:32,919 --> 00:08:42,600
like is the integral of the gradient of p-theta

164
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:47,360
of tau r of tau

165
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:50,620
Now, this doesn't really help us directly in any way

166
00:08:50,620 --> 00:08:53,360
because we don't want to have to evaluate this integral in order

167
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:55,200
to take a gradient

168
00:08:55,200 --> 00:09:01,120
And at this point, there is kind of a trick, a convenient trick

169
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:02,640
that we can use to try to put this

170
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:07,240
into a form that's more useful

171
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:11,800
And in particular, the trick is that--

172
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:14,760
we'll write it over here--

173
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:21,680
is that if you think about p-theta

174
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:32,400
of tau times the gradient of p-theta of tau-- actually

175
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:41,480
sorry, times the gradient of the log

176
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:44,680
Does anyone remember what the gradient of a log is

177
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:46,060
or the derivative of log

178
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:50,910
[INAUDIBLE] 1 over, right

179
00:09:50,910 --> 00:09:53,350
Then what this is equal to

180
00:09:53,350 --> 00:10:03,370
This is equal to p-theta of tau times 1 over p-theta of tau

181
00:10:03,370 --> 00:10:04,950
And then we need to use the chain rule

182
00:10:04,950 --> 00:10:06,700
to apply the gradient to this

183
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:08,070
And so with the chain rule, we'll

184
00:10:08,070 --> 00:10:13,510
get the gradient of p-theta of tau

185
00:10:13,510 --> 00:10:15,910
And these two will then cancel out

186
00:10:15,910 --> 00:10:22,390
And this is equal to the gradient of p-theta of tau

187
00:10:22,390 --> 00:10:26,750
And the reason why this is a convenient equality between

188
00:10:26,750 --> 00:10:28,090
gradient of p-theta--

189
00:10:28,090 --> 00:10:29,750
and this is we could actually then

190
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:34,990
replace the gradient of p-theta down here with this first thing

191
00:10:34,990 --> 00:10:36,590
right here

192
00:10:36,590 --> 00:10:41,590
If we replace this gradient of p-theta of tau right here

193
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:49,630
we replace this, we now get an integral of p-theta of tau times

194
00:10:49,630 --> 00:11:03,430
grad log p-theta of tau times r of tau d tau

195
00:11:03,430 --> 00:11:05,630
Now, this is something that's useful

196
00:11:05,630 --> 00:11:07,030
because we can now represent this

197
00:11:07,030 --> 00:11:10,550
as an expectation, this first term right here

198
00:11:10,550 --> 00:11:14,230
And so this is equal to an expectation

199
00:11:14,230 --> 00:11:22,440
of tau sampled from p-theta of tau of grad log

200
00:11:22,440 --> 00:11:30,710
p-theta of tau times r of tau

201
00:11:30,710 --> 00:11:34,310
So what we did there is we used this log trick

202
00:11:34,310 --> 00:11:38,590
to then get the p-theta out here and then get an expectation

203
00:11:38,590 --> 00:11:41,830
And we're starting to get to something that's actually more

204
00:11:41,830 --> 00:11:43,430
what we can evaluate

205
00:11:43,430 --> 00:11:45,620
And in particular, this expectation

206
00:11:45,620 --> 00:11:47,980
is something that we can estimate with samples

207
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:52,540
And now theta is actually appearing on the inside here

208
00:11:52,540 --> 00:11:55,460
And that means that this is actually

209
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:57,700
getting closer to something that we can evaluate

210
00:11:57,700 --> 00:12:00,160
We still have this term right here

211
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:03,380
which doesn't look like pi of a given S

212
00:12:03,380 --> 00:12:05,390
So we need to work on this term

213
00:12:05,390 --> 00:12:07,180
But we're starting to get towards something

214
00:12:07,180 --> 00:12:09,300
that we can evaluate

215
00:12:09,300 --> 00:12:12,480
So the question is, how is this more useful than the first term

216
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:14,220
because we still have the sampling

217
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:17,220
and the issue with the first case

218
00:12:17,220 --> 00:12:20,200
was that we can't differentiate through the sampling

219
00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:21,700
Now, the thing that's different here

220
00:12:21,700 --> 00:12:24,740
is that theta actually appears inside the expectation

221
00:12:24,740 --> 00:12:26,700
Now we're not going to be able to differentiate

222
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:29,420
through the sampling, still

223
00:12:29,420 --> 00:12:31,420
But the thing that's useful here is

224
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:34,420
that we will actually be able to differentiate

225
00:12:34,420 --> 00:12:36,220
with respect to theta

226
00:12:36,220 --> 00:12:39,380
And we're still not going to differentiate in this part

227
00:12:39,380 --> 00:12:42,240
but we'll be able to get an estimate that's still useful

228
00:12:42,240 --> 00:12:45,620
And we could potentially draw many, many samples

229
00:12:45,620 --> 00:12:47,740
And then measure this quantity to get actually

230
00:12:47,740 --> 00:12:52,570
a pretty accurate representation of the gradient in a way that

231
00:12:52,570 --> 00:12:57,640
actually depends on theta

232
00:12:57,640 --> 00:12:58,140
OK

233
00:12:58,140 --> 00:13:03,570
Let's talk a little bit about this term right here

234
00:13:03,570 --> 00:13:06,420
And actually, maybe in case it's useful in terms

235
00:13:06,420 --> 00:13:09,960
of where we're at so far, we can show that on the slides as well

236
00:13:09,960 --> 00:13:16,820
So we looked at this j of theta, wrote it down as an integral

237
00:13:16,820 --> 00:13:19,900
We found this convenient identity over here

238
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:23,700
where p grad log p is equal to grad p

239
00:13:23,700 --> 00:13:27,540
And then we can use that to derive this second equation

240
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:30,660
right here

241
00:13:30,660 --> 00:13:33,340
And now we're right here

242
00:13:33,340 --> 00:13:37,860
So for this part, for grad log p

243
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:40,060
this is something that we can write out

244
00:13:40,060 --> 00:13:41,780
And if we use--

245
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:51,570
if we remember that p-theta of tau equals p of S1 times pi

246
00:13:51,570 --> 00:13:59,490
of at given St and P of St plus 1, given St at

247
00:13:59,490 --> 00:14:06,090
and then with a product over t

248
00:14:06,090 --> 00:14:10,290
Then when we take the log of this, it's over there

249
00:14:10,290 --> 00:14:19,170
the log of p-theta of tau is equal to the log

250
00:14:19,170 --> 00:14:35,090
of p of S1 plus the sum over T of the log of pi plus the log

251
00:14:35,090 --> 00:14:41,930
of the dynamics

252
00:14:41,930 --> 00:14:49,890
And then once we actually take the gradient of log p tau, then

253
00:14:49,890 --> 00:14:52,290
the gradient of this with respect to theta

254
00:14:52,290 --> 00:14:54,870
theta doesn't affect the initial state distribution

255
00:14:54,870 --> 00:14:57,450
It also doesn't affect the dynamics in any way

256
00:14:57,450 --> 00:15:05,570
And so the gradient of log p-theta of tau

257
00:15:05,570 --> 00:15:09,650
is equal to just the gradient of this term right here

258
00:15:09,650 --> 00:15:24,060
So the gradient of log pi-theta of a given S

259
00:15:24,060 --> 00:15:25,770
And so this is really useful because this

260
00:15:25,770 --> 00:15:31,180
means that we can actually plug in this over there

261
00:15:31,180 --> 00:15:33,350
And this is actually something that we can evaluate

262
00:15:33,350 --> 00:15:36,050
We can evaluate the gradient of--

263
00:15:36,050 --> 00:15:41,040
or the log-- the gradient of log pi

264
00:15:41,040 --> 00:15:43,400
And this is just taking a backward pass

265
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:50,240
through the log of the output of our neural network

266
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:55,120
And so then we actually get a gradient that we can evaluate

267
00:15:55,120 --> 00:16:08,320
So the gradient that we get when we plug things in

268
00:16:08,320 --> 00:16:13,600
the gradient of j-theta when we plug this in

269
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:19,840
is we have an expectation over trajectories

270
00:16:19,840 --> 00:16:23,280
sampled from our policy

271
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:27,440
And inside that expectation, we can bring the gradient

272
00:16:27,440 --> 00:16:29,360
inside that summation

273
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:35,600
And so we'll have a sum over time steps of grad log

274
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:41,000
pi of at given St, right here

275
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,800
And then we still have our r of t

276
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:47,320
right here, so we can write that as r of tau

277
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:58,760
We could also write it as a summation of r of St comma at

278
00:16:58,760 --> 00:17:02,320
So this is what's called the policy gradient or the vanilla

279
00:17:02,320 --> 00:17:03,920
policy gradient

280
00:17:03,920 --> 00:17:07,960
And this is a lot nicer than the version

281
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:13,079
up there because we can sample trajectories from our policy

282
00:17:13,079 --> 00:17:14,560
compute their reward

283
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:19,319
Also, compute the gradient of each of log

284
00:17:19,319 --> 00:17:21,760
of pi for each of the actions

285
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:25,880
And then sum those together, multiply

286
00:17:25,880 --> 00:17:27,760
and we can get a gradient that we

287
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:30,720
can apply to the weights of our neural network

288
00:17:30,720 --> 00:17:36,200
That's actually leading to a higher reward

289
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:41,710
So again, on the slides, we wrote down

290
00:17:41,710 --> 00:17:44,070
what the probability of a trajectory is

291
00:17:44,070 --> 00:17:46,790
If you take the log of that, you get

292
00:17:46,790 --> 00:17:49,950
a summation instead of a product

293
00:17:49,950 --> 00:17:53,190
and the log of each of the individual terms

294
00:17:53,190 --> 00:17:59,630
Then if we plug that into our gradient

295
00:17:59,630 --> 00:18:03,110
and we take the gradient of log p tau

296
00:18:03,110 --> 00:18:06,230
then the first term and the last term

297
00:18:06,230 --> 00:18:10,630
go away because they don't depend on theta

298
00:18:10,630 --> 00:18:18,190
And then we get this final term when we plug it in

299
00:18:18,190 --> 00:18:19,530
So this is the gradient

300
00:18:19,530 --> 00:18:21,830
When we run policy gradient, we'll collect data

301
00:18:21,830 --> 00:18:25,070
And then we'll evaluate this equation

302
00:18:25,070 --> 00:18:28,270
And then we'll use that to represent the gradient

303
00:18:28,270 --> 00:18:30,310
of the weights of the policy

304
00:18:30,310 --> 00:18:34,190
And then run gradient descent, apply that to our policy

305
00:18:34,190 --> 00:18:41,030
and then repeat the process

306
00:18:41,030 --> 00:18:43,370
So yeah, we also talked about this verbally

307
00:18:43,370 --> 00:18:45,550
But in order to estimate this gradient here

308
00:18:45,550 --> 00:18:47,630
we'll run the policy to collect some data

309
00:18:47,630 --> 00:18:50,890
We'll then improve the policy using that data

310
00:18:50,890 --> 00:18:55,590
In particular, you can think of the first summation

311
00:18:55,590 --> 00:18:59,410
as collecting n samples for that expectation

312
00:18:59,410 --> 00:19:01,890
And ideally, n is larger because the higher n

313
00:19:01,890 --> 00:19:03,270
is, the better an estimate you're

314
00:19:03,270 --> 00:19:07,990
going to get of the expectation of the integral

315
00:19:07,990 --> 00:19:10,150
And then once we evaluate this gradient

316
00:19:10,150 --> 00:19:12,210
that we're estimating with samples

317
00:19:12,210 --> 00:19:18,600
we'll then take a gradient step on our policy parameters

318
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:20,310
And so the full algorithm looks something

319
00:19:20,310 --> 00:19:23,630
like sampling trajectories from our policy

320
00:19:23,630 --> 00:19:30,230
evaluating an estimate of the gradient using those samples

321
00:19:30,230 --> 00:19:34,070
applying that to the weights, and then repeating this process

322
00:19:34,070 --> 00:19:37,780
to collect a new batch of data, recomputing the gradient

323
00:19:37,780 --> 00:19:40,200
and improving the policy

324
00:19:40,200 --> 00:19:41,740
So the question is, what does it look

325
00:19:41,740 --> 00:19:46,170
like to use the data to estimate this gradient term--

326
00:19:46,170 --> 00:19:49,260
the first term or the second term

327
00:19:49,260 --> 00:19:50,560
The first term

328
00:19:50,560 --> 00:19:51,060
Yeah

329
00:19:51,060 --> 00:19:56,240
So when we're using the data to compute this first term, what

330
00:19:56,240 --> 00:19:58,700
we're doing is we're getting a bunch of states and actions

331
00:19:58,700 --> 00:19:59,890
a bunch of trajectories

332
00:19:59,890 --> 00:20:02,060
We're getting n trajectories at each-- in this case

333
00:20:02,060 --> 00:20:04,140
have length t time steps

334
00:20:04,140 --> 00:20:06,820
And we'll be taking the states and actions

335
00:20:06,820 --> 00:20:09,540
in those trajectories, passing the state

336
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:12,860
through the policy as a forward pass

337
00:20:12,860 --> 00:20:14,740
to get the probability of our actions

338
00:20:14,740 --> 00:20:19,430
We'll then evaluate the action in the data, the probability

339
00:20:19,430 --> 00:20:23,380
of that action in the data under the distribution, the policy

340
00:20:23,380 --> 00:20:23,920
output

341
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:26,890
So it's like a categorical distribution

342
00:20:26,890 --> 00:20:28,180
We'll then get the probability

343
00:20:28,180 --> 00:20:30,450
We'll take the log of that probability

344
00:20:30,450 --> 00:20:32,160
And then we take the gradient right here

345
00:20:32,160 --> 00:20:33,720
which will just be a forward pass--

346
00:20:33,720 --> 00:20:36,900
sorry, a backward pass through the network

347
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:41,700
after taking the log of the policy output for that action

348
00:20:41,700 --> 00:20:44,500
So this is called the REINFORCE algorithm or the vanilla policy

349
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:46,670
gradient algorithm

350
00:20:46,670 --> 00:20:47,380
So this came out

351
00:20:47,380 --> 00:20:49,220
This is just r of tau

352
00:20:49,220 --> 00:20:52,940
So I'm using r of tau as shorthand for this

353
00:20:52,940 --> 00:20:57,460
just because I didn't want to write the summation five times

354
00:20:57,460 --> 00:21:00,780
But yeah, this is exactly r of tau

355
00:21:00,780 --> 00:21:01,360
Yeah, exactly

356
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:03,070
So this is an estimate because we're only

357
00:21:03,070 --> 00:21:05,840
going to be taking n samples to estimate this expectation

358
00:21:05,840 --> 00:21:11,200
If you took infinite, then this would be exact

359
00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:13,330
Of course, taking infinite samples is not possible

360
00:21:13,330 --> 00:21:14,120
The gradient

361
00:21:14,120 --> 00:21:14,620
Yeah

362
00:21:14,620 --> 00:21:16,330
So the gradient right here, this is going

363
00:21:16,330 --> 00:21:18,500
to depend on the architecture

364
00:21:18,500 --> 00:21:21,700
The question was, do we compute this by hand

365
00:21:21,700 --> 00:21:24,200
You can use automatic differentiation

366
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:26,160
So whatever library, PyTorch or whatever

367
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:27,700
that can compute that for you, so you

368
00:21:27,700 --> 00:21:30,220
don't have to write it out

369
00:21:30,220 --> 00:21:30,720
Great

370
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:32,970
So let's talk about some of the intuition behind this

371
00:21:32,970 --> 00:21:34,330
So there's the math

372
00:21:34,330 --> 00:21:37,530
But what does this gradient actually mean

373
00:21:37,530 --> 00:21:39,970
So if you look at this, maybe let's

374
00:21:39,970 --> 00:21:42,170
start by looking at the first term

375
00:21:42,170 --> 00:21:48,170
The first term if we think back to imitation learning

376
00:21:48,170 --> 00:21:50,770
this first term is actually the same as the gradient

377
00:21:50,770 --> 00:21:52,270
of the imitation learning objective

378
00:21:52,270 --> 00:21:53,770
So in imitation learning, we were

379
00:21:53,770 --> 00:21:57,730
trying to mimic actions from some demonstration data set

380
00:21:57,730 --> 00:22:00,570
by maximizing the log probability

381
00:22:00,570 --> 00:22:03,730
or minimizing the negative log probability

382
00:22:03,730 --> 00:22:05,390
And if you take the gradient of that

383
00:22:05,390 --> 00:22:10,570
it's similarly going to be the gradient of the log probability

384
00:22:10,570 --> 00:22:12,610
So you can think about this first term as saying

385
00:22:12,610 --> 00:22:15,810
I'm going to basically try to increase

386
00:22:15,810 --> 00:22:21,290
the likelihood of the actions in the online data

387
00:22:21,290 --> 00:22:23,370
But then for the second term, it's

388
00:22:23,370 --> 00:22:27,150
weighting that by the reward of that trajectory

389
00:22:27,150 --> 00:22:30,210
How good was that trajectory

390
00:22:30,210 --> 00:22:32,870
And so you can think about this as the imitation gradient

391
00:22:32,870 --> 00:22:38,010
but weighted by the sum of rewards seen in the data

392
00:22:38,010 --> 00:22:39,970
And so essentially, you're going to increase

393
00:22:39,970 --> 00:22:43,090
the likelihood of actions that had high reward

394
00:22:43,090 --> 00:22:46,250
and decrease the likelihood of actions

395
00:22:46,250 --> 00:22:49,950
that were taken in negative reward trajectories

396
00:22:49,950 --> 00:22:51,370
And so you're actually going to be

397
00:22:51,370 --> 00:22:53,490
imitating your own data, the data that

398
00:22:53,490 --> 00:22:56,330
was collected by the policy

399
00:22:56,330 --> 00:23:02,630
But depending on whether it's high reward or low reward

400
00:23:02,630 --> 00:23:05,340
you actually might have a negative gradient

401
00:23:05,340 --> 00:23:07,010
If it's negative reward, that's actually

402
00:23:07,010 --> 00:23:08,770
going to push you away from the bad stuff

403
00:23:08,770 --> 00:23:10,760
and push you towards the good stuff

404
00:23:10,760 --> 00:23:12,470
[INAUDIBLE] Yeah

405
00:23:12,470 --> 00:23:14,510
So this is inside the first summation

406
00:23:14,510 --> 00:23:16,500
You can see the eye comes up right here

407
00:23:16,500 --> 00:23:18,170
So we're summing a bunch of directories

408
00:23:18,170 --> 00:23:21,630
and then, yeah, this is all within the summation over n

409
00:23:21,630 --> 00:23:23,170
So the question is, is the difference

410
00:23:23,170 --> 00:23:24,930
between this and imitation learning where

411
00:23:24,930 --> 00:23:26,810
imitation learning you're either right or wrong

412
00:23:26,810 --> 00:23:28,520
or here you're like, this amount of right

413
00:23:28,520 --> 00:23:31,360
or this amount of wrong

414
00:23:31,360 --> 00:23:34,120
So you can apply this--

415
00:23:34,120 --> 00:23:36,640
well, in principle, you can apply this in scenarios

416
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:38,600
where the reward is very sparse

417
00:23:38,600 --> 00:23:40,560
And so you can actually apply it in scenarios

418
00:23:40,560 --> 00:23:44,080
where the reward is just 1 if you succeeded, and 0 otherwise

419
00:23:44,080 --> 00:23:46,860
Now, this algorithm may not do very well in those scenarios

420
00:23:46,860 --> 00:23:48,520
and we'll talk about that

421
00:23:48,520 --> 00:23:50,600
One of the key differences with imitation learning

422
00:23:50,600 --> 00:23:53,460
is that you're actually doing this on your own rollouts

423
00:23:53,460 --> 00:23:55,860
Actually, the policy is collecting its own data

424
00:23:55,860 --> 00:23:59,440
and then applying this objective to its own data

425
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:00,980
And so that's one of the differences

426
00:24:00,980 --> 00:24:02,360
And then second is we're actually

427
00:24:02,360 --> 00:24:04,600
using this reward, whereas in imitation learning

428
00:24:04,600 --> 00:24:07,880
you're just blindly imitating all the data without any notion

429
00:24:07,880 --> 00:24:09,420
of how good the data is

430
00:24:09,420 --> 00:24:11,740
And it assumes that it's good data

431
00:24:11,740 --> 00:24:12,240
Yeah

432
00:24:12,240 --> 00:24:16,940
How do you sample the first state of the trajectory

433
00:24:16,940 --> 00:24:18,400
This is something that's typically

434
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:20,780
defined by the problem setting

435
00:24:20,780 --> 00:24:23,400
So in some problem settings, if you're

436
00:24:23,400 --> 00:24:27,780
trying to train an agent to play pinball or something

437
00:24:27,780 --> 00:24:33,200
then the initial state will just be given by the pinball machine

438
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:36,240
In other cases, in language modeling

439
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:39,760
the initial state or observation might be the first question

440
00:24:39,760 --> 00:24:41,480
that the user asked

441
00:24:41,480 --> 00:24:45,060
So it's usually thought of as a property of the environment

442
00:24:45,060 --> 00:24:46,560
So the question is, can you use this

443
00:24:46,560 --> 00:24:48,920
to improve imitation learning by weighting

444
00:24:48,920 --> 00:24:50,520
the trajectories by reward

445
00:24:50,520 --> 00:24:52,120
Yeah, you could definitely do that

446
00:24:52,120 --> 00:24:56,200
We will also talk about some of the good things and bad things

447
00:24:56,200 --> 00:24:59,290
about this objective in the coming slides

448
00:24:59,290 --> 00:25:00,660
So the question was, in practice

449
00:25:00,660 --> 00:25:02,300
does it make sense to start with imitation learning

450
00:25:02,300 --> 00:25:03,920
run that for some number of iterations

451
00:25:03,920 --> 00:25:06,160
and then switch to this

452
00:25:06,160 --> 00:25:07,680
That can definitely make sense

453
00:25:07,680 --> 00:25:10,760
If you start completely from a randomly initialized policy

454
00:25:10,760 --> 00:25:12,600
it will do random stuff

455
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:16,320
And if you have a very dense reward signal, that can be OK

456
00:25:16,320 --> 00:25:19,420
But in other scenarios, if you have access to demonstrations

457
00:25:19,420 --> 00:25:22,060
then initializing it with demonstrations

458
00:25:22,060 --> 00:25:25,160
can be a very good fit, and then it

459
00:25:25,160 --> 00:25:30,080
will allow you to improve on top of your initial policy

460
00:25:30,080 --> 00:25:32,790
So the question is, if you don't have a good conception of what's

461
00:25:32,790 --> 00:25:35,910
good and bad, what the reward function should be

462
00:25:35,910 --> 00:25:37,950
can you use imitation learning to get

463
00:25:37,950 --> 00:25:39,790
an initial sense for that

464
00:25:39,790 --> 00:25:42,990
We'll talk about reward learning in a few lectures

465
00:25:42,990 --> 00:25:47,150
and how you can potentially use expert data for that

466
00:25:47,150 --> 00:25:52,190
In practice, it's challenging

467
00:25:52,190 --> 00:25:54,950
So we talked about how this intuitively increasing

468
00:25:54,950 --> 00:25:57,990
likelihood on high reward trajectories

469
00:25:57,990 --> 00:26:01,430
and decreasing likelihood on negative reward trajectories

470
00:26:01,430 --> 00:26:04,050
If we actually look at this visually

471
00:26:04,050 --> 00:26:06,050
say that we took a few trajectories--

472
00:26:06,050 --> 00:26:08,530
one of them had good reward, one of them had bad reward--

473
00:26:08,530 --> 00:26:12,310
and this red is showing our initial distribution

474
00:26:12,310 --> 00:26:14,790
over trajectories, what this gradient is going to do

475
00:26:14,790 --> 00:26:17,410
is it's going to increase the likelihood on the green part

476
00:26:17,410 --> 00:26:19,390
So you'll get something that looks like this

477
00:26:19,390 --> 00:26:21,490
And then it'll decrease the likelihood on the red part

478
00:26:21,490 --> 00:26:23,370
so you'll get something that looks like this

479
00:26:23,370 --> 00:26:25,910
And it'll encourage the policy to take

480
00:26:25,910 --> 00:26:28,500
actions that are more like the high-reward trajectory

481
00:26:28,500 --> 00:26:32,270
[INAUDIBLE]

482
00:26:32,270 --> 00:26:35,390
In even more brief terms, you can think of this

483
00:26:35,390 --> 00:26:39,830
as trying to do more of the good stuff and less of the bad stuff

484
00:26:39,830 --> 00:26:41,770
that you observe

485
00:26:41,770 --> 00:26:43,350
And then you can also think about this

486
00:26:43,350 --> 00:26:46,410
as a formalization of trial and error

487
00:26:46,410 --> 00:26:50,170
where you're actually going to try to do different things

488
00:26:50,170 --> 00:26:52,690
And if you see good stuff happening

489
00:26:52,690 --> 00:26:54,170
then you'll try to do more of that

490
00:26:54,170 --> 00:26:58,110
And if you make a mistake, then you'll try to stop doing that

491
00:26:58,110 --> 00:26:59,290
Cool

492
00:26:59,290 --> 00:27:02,550
So now I have a question for you

493
00:27:02,550 --> 00:27:04,130
So let's look at an example

494
00:27:04,130 --> 00:27:05,910
Say that we're trying to have a humanoid

495
00:27:05,910 --> 00:27:08,790
learn how to walk in simulation, and we'll

496
00:27:08,790 --> 00:27:11,110
define the reward function to be the forward velocity

497
00:27:11,110 --> 00:27:12,040
of the robot

498
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:14,210
This means that the reward function will be positive

499
00:27:14,210 --> 00:27:15,890
if it's moving forward

500
00:27:15,890 --> 00:27:18,870
It may also be negative if the robot is moving backwards

501
00:27:18,870 --> 00:27:23,350
It might be 0 if the robot is not moving

502
00:27:23,350 --> 00:27:25,820
First, we'll sample from our policy

503
00:27:25,820 --> 00:27:28,660
Say we have a policy that's not very good at the beginning

504
00:27:28,660 --> 00:27:31,380
So in the first trajectory, maybe it falls backwards

505
00:27:31,380 --> 00:27:33,620
In the second trajectory, maybe it

506
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:36,140
falls forward, which will actually technically give it

507
00:27:36,140 --> 00:27:37,380
positive reward

508
00:27:37,380 --> 00:27:40,980
So the sum of rewards will be positive

509
00:27:40,980 --> 00:27:43,460
Maybe another trajectory, it manages to stand still

510
00:27:43,460 --> 00:27:46,460
So the rewards would be 0 for that

511
00:27:46,460 --> 00:27:49,900
In another trajectory, maybe it takes one small step forward

512
00:27:49,900 --> 00:27:51,460
and then falls backwards

513
00:27:51,460 --> 00:27:55,420
And so the initial reward at the beginning of the trajectory

514
00:27:55,420 --> 00:27:57,320
would actually be getting positive rewards

515
00:27:57,320 --> 00:27:59,650
And then later on, it would be getting negative rewards

516
00:27:59,650 --> 00:28:02,060
and the sum of rewards would be negative

517
00:28:02,060 --> 00:28:03,500
And then maybe the last trajectory

518
00:28:03,500 --> 00:28:05,580
is it takes one large step backwards

519
00:28:05,580 --> 00:28:07,480
and then a small step forwards

520
00:28:07,480 --> 00:28:11,260
And in this case, it would have negative rewards, and then

521
00:28:11,260 --> 00:28:13,540
some positive rewards, but the sum of rewards

522
00:28:13,540 --> 00:28:16,500
would still be negative

523
00:28:16,500 --> 00:28:22,340
Now my question for you is, what will the gradient that we

524
00:28:22,340 --> 00:28:24,900
defined here, what will it encourage the policy

525
00:28:24,900 --> 00:28:28,380
to do if this is your data set, if these are the five

526
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:33,440
trajectories that you sampled

527
00:28:33,440 --> 00:28:35,440
This is going to be one of our simpler examples

528
00:28:35,440 --> 00:28:37,500
and then we'll move to more complex examples

529
00:28:37,500 --> 00:28:39,220
in a couple more slides

530
00:28:39,220 --> 00:28:41,520
So I'd encourage you to pair up for a minute

531
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:43,220
discuss it briefly, and then we'll

532
00:28:43,220 --> 00:28:47,020
come back and share in 1 or 2 minutes

533
00:28:47,020 --> 00:28:49,420
OK, let's come back

534
00:28:49,420 --> 00:28:54,700
If you want to share your answer, can you raise your hand

535
00:28:54,700 --> 00:28:55,200
OK

536
00:28:55,200 --> 00:28:56,580
So the gradient will encourage it

