So that is a subset, but it might not be--

804
00:41:28,600 --> 00:41:30,220
and I guess-- in the next lecture

805
00:41:30,220 --> 00:41:35,080
we'll talk about more sophisticated ways to estimate

806
00:41:35,080 --> 00:41:39,860
this-- are smarter basically

807
00:41:39,860 --> 00:41:40,360
Cool

808
00:41:40,360 --> 00:41:42,190
So the thing that we saw here is that things

809
00:41:42,190 --> 00:41:44,380
are sensitive to the scaling of the reward function

810
00:41:44,380 --> 00:41:46,460
And on the flip side, if everything was negative--

811
00:41:46,460 --> 00:41:48,320
it came up in the question before--

812
00:41:48,320 --> 00:41:50,880
we would actually only be getting negative gradients

813
00:41:50,880 --> 00:41:53,640
to our policy and no actual positive gradients

814
00:41:53,640 --> 00:41:56,550
And that's actually a big problem

815
00:41:56,550 --> 00:41:59,600
with this kind of approach is that it depends

816
00:41:59,600 --> 00:42:01,850
on the scale of the reward

817
00:42:01,850 --> 00:42:03,520
And there's actually a very simple thing

818
00:42:03,520 --> 00:42:06,470
that we can do to fix this

819
00:42:06,470 --> 00:42:09,550
And so in particular, what we can do

820
00:42:09,550 --> 00:42:13,550
is when we are using this gradient, which is

821
00:42:13,550 --> 00:42:20,350
the expectation of p of tau--

822
00:42:20,350 --> 00:42:24,710
I'll maybe do the first version just to make it a bit shorter

823
00:42:24,710 --> 00:42:32,350
but we get the gradient of log p of tau times r of tau

824
00:42:32,350 --> 00:42:35,790
And what we can do is instead of having this be r of tau

825
00:42:35,790 --> 00:42:40,230
we can actually subtract a constant

826
00:42:40,230 --> 00:42:50,670
where that constant could be equal to the average reward

827
00:42:50,670 --> 00:42:53,560
And if we subtract the average reward from this

828
00:42:53,560 --> 00:42:58,510
this means that things that are better than average

829
00:42:58,510 --> 00:43:00,190
will increase the likelihood, and things

830
00:43:00,190 --> 00:43:03,550
that are below average will actually

831
00:43:03,550 --> 00:43:05,390
lead us to decrease likelihood

832
00:43:05,390 --> 00:43:08,030
So this will be negative

833
00:43:08,030 --> 00:43:13,310
Now, you might be wondering, can we just add this b here

834
00:43:13,310 --> 00:43:16,150
Did I just make that up

835
00:43:16,150 --> 00:43:17,070
Why can we do that

836
00:43:17,070 --> 00:43:23,470
Why are we allowed to do that

837
00:43:23,470 --> 00:43:27,870
We can see what the effect that this has on the gradient

838
00:43:27,870 --> 00:43:30,790
And what we can show is we can actually

839
00:43:30,790 --> 00:43:35,470
show that if you subtract a constant in expectation

840
00:43:35,470 --> 00:43:38,470
this has zero effect on the gradient

841
00:43:38,470 --> 00:43:45,950
And in particular-- so this is the gradient of J

842
00:43:45,950 --> 00:43:49,750
And if we look at what effect this has on the gradient

843
00:43:49,750 --> 00:43:54,710
and expectation, we can look at essentially the expectation

844
00:43:54,710 --> 00:44:07,540
of grad log p times b

845
00:44:07,540 --> 00:44:11,900
And it's possible to show that this in expectation

846
00:44:11,900 --> 00:44:14,380
is actually equal to zero

847
00:44:14,380 --> 00:44:23,100
And in particular, if we write this out as the gradient of--

848
00:44:23,100 --> 00:44:26,180
or sorry, the expectation is the integral

849
00:44:26,180 --> 00:44:39,460
of p of tau times grad log p of tau times bd tau

850
00:44:39,460 --> 00:44:43,340
We can then use the same trick that we

851
00:44:43,340 --> 00:44:45,500
used before, but in reverse

852
00:44:45,500 --> 00:44:59,140
This is equal to the integral of grad p-theta of tau times bd

853
00:44:59,140 --> 00:45:01,700
tau

854
00:45:01,700 --> 00:45:05,180
I'm a little bit out of space, but the gradient

855
00:45:05,180 --> 00:45:09,400
can be moved to the outside of the integral

856
00:45:09,400 --> 00:45:13,300
because this is a linear operation

857
00:45:13,300 --> 00:45:16,100
We can also move b outside the integral

858
00:45:16,100 --> 00:45:17,940
because it's a constant

859
00:45:17,940 --> 00:45:29,140
And so this is equal to the gradient

860
00:45:29,140 --> 00:45:39,660
b times the gradient of the integral of p-theta of tau d

861
00:45:39,660 --> 00:45:41,100
tau

862
00:45:41,100 --> 00:45:45,860
Now this integral is just equal to 1, and the gradient of 1

863
00:45:45,860 --> 00:45:47,540
is 0

864
00:45:47,540 --> 00:45:57,180
So this is 1, this is 0, and b times 0 is 0

865
00:45:57,180 --> 00:46:01,290
And so this means that the expectation, this

866
00:46:01,290 --> 00:46:05,570
is going to have 0 effect on the gradient

867
00:46:05,570 --> 00:46:11,490
And so just to maybe write that out a little bit more cleanly

868
00:46:11,490 --> 00:46:15,490
the expectation of the gradient of log p times

869
00:46:15,490 --> 00:46:17,370
b-- so basically, the fact that this baseline

870
00:46:17,370 --> 00:46:19,690
is having on this gradient

871
00:46:19,690 --> 00:46:22,170
We can write that as the integral

872
00:46:22,170 --> 00:46:25,170
We can then use the log trick in reverse

873
00:46:25,170 --> 00:46:30,450
We can move b and the gradient outside of the integral

874
00:46:30,450 --> 00:46:33,890
This integral is equal to 1, the gradient of 1 is 0

875
00:46:33,890 --> 00:46:35,650
and then we get 0

876
00:46:35,650 --> 00:46:38,410
So this means that we can add this baseline

877
00:46:38,410 --> 00:46:40,650
So the reason why this constant term-- the question

878
00:46:40,650 --> 00:46:43,930
was this constant term wouldn't be helpful if we could actually

879
00:46:43,930 --> 00:46:45,870
truly evaluate the expectation

880
00:46:45,870 --> 00:46:46,910
And yeah, that's correct

881
00:46:46,910 --> 00:46:50,410
So if we had this expectation and could measure this exactly

882
00:46:50,410 --> 00:46:51,580
then we wouldn't need this

883
00:46:51,580 --> 00:46:53,410
The reason why this is helpful is because we

884
00:46:53,410 --> 00:46:56,610
have this expectation

885
00:46:56,610 --> 00:46:58,910
Basically, you can think of it as reducing--

886
00:46:58,910 --> 00:47:01,490
it's a way to reduce the variance of your estimate

887
00:47:01,490 --> 00:47:06,090
of this expectation, and it's an unbiased way to do that

888
00:47:06,090 --> 00:47:11,970
So in particular, as long as the baseline is a constant

889
00:47:11,970 --> 00:47:13,450
then it won't change the behavior

890
00:47:13,450 --> 00:47:14,740
of the gradient in expectation

891
00:47:14,740 --> 00:47:16,270
This means that it's unbiased

892
00:47:16,270 --> 00:47:20,170
This is a term from statistics, talking about effect

893
00:47:20,170 --> 00:47:21,470
on random variables

894
00:47:21,470 --> 00:47:24,690
And you can show that it reduces the variance of your estimate

895
00:47:24,690 --> 00:47:26,220
of the gradient

896
00:47:26,220 --> 00:47:27,970
So the question is, is b really a constant

897
00:47:27,970 --> 00:47:30,890
and seems to depend on the trajectories

898
00:47:30,890 --> 00:47:36,970
We'll use the same value of b for an entire batch

899
00:47:36,970 --> 00:47:38,830
for all of the examples

900
00:47:38,830 --> 00:47:43,010
And so we'll collect a batch of data, then we will compute b

901
00:47:43,010 --> 00:47:47,770
and we'll then use b for all of the updates

902
00:47:47,770 --> 00:47:49,810
on that batch of data

903
00:47:49,810 --> 00:47:53,210
So how does it reduce the variance of the gradient

904
00:47:53,210 --> 00:47:54,710
You can write down--

905
00:47:54,710 --> 00:47:56,820
so this is the expectation of the gradient

906
00:47:56,820 --> 00:47:58,680
You can write down the variance analytically

907
00:47:58,680 --> 00:48:03,550
and show that it reduces the variance

908
00:48:03,550 --> 00:48:05,520
I don't want to go through the math

909
00:48:05,520 --> 00:48:07,560
because we have some other stuff to get through

910
00:48:07,560 --> 00:48:09,320
But intuitively, you can think about it

911
00:48:09,320 --> 00:48:14,000
in terms of this example of--

912
00:48:14,000 --> 00:48:17,890
it will encourage it to do more of the above-average stuff

913
00:48:17,890 --> 00:48:21,910
and less of the below-average stuff

914
00:48:21,910 --> 00:48:24,280
I could also mention that average reward is actually

915
00:48:24,280 --> 00:48:26,000
not the optimal baseline

916
00:48:26,000 --> 00:48:29,520
You can actually derive the variance

917
00:48:29,520 --> 00:48:34,360
and then take the derivative of the variance side equal to 0

918
00:48:34,360 --> 00:48:36,840
to find the optimal baseline

919
00:48:36,840 --> 00:48:38,600
But average reward is pretty good

920
00:48:38,600 --> 00:48:41,380
The optimal baseline is something--

921
00:48:41,380 --> 00:48:42,860
it's very close to average reward

922
00:48:42,860 --> 00:48:47,160
but there's some additional weighting terms

923
00:48:47,160 --> 00:48:48,120
Cool

924
00:48:48,120 --> 00:48:52,360
So we have one more episode of what does the gradient do

925
00:48:52,360 --> 00:48:55,280
And we're going to look at a different example

926
00:48:55,280 --> 00:48:59,040
In this case, the robot is folding a jacket

927
00:48:59,040 --> 00:49:01,880
And the reward is 1 if it's neatly folded

928
00:49:01,880 --> 00:49:04,300
0.5 if it's folded with some wrinkles

929
00:49:04,300 --> 00:49:07,200
and 0 if it's not folded

930
00:49:07,200 --> 00:49:09,600
We're going to again sample from our policy

931
00:49:09,600 --> 00:49:12,020
We have some samples of not touching the jacket

932
00:49:12,020 --> 00:49:15,080
folding only the sleeves but not folding the full thing

933
00:49:15,080 --> 00:49:17,140
flattening the jacket but not folding it

934
00:49:17,140 --> 00:49:20,440
and one trajectory where it does fold the jacket

935
00:49:20,440 --> 00:49:24,480
And my question is, with the baseline in here this time

936
00:49:24,480 --> 00:49:26,880
what does the gradient look like

937
00:49:26,880 --> 00:49:32,960
The rewards for 1, 2, and 3 will be 0, and the reward for 4

938
00:49:32,960 --> 00:49:34,340
will be positive

939
00:49:34,340 --> 00:49:36,300
And so this means that because of our baseline

940
00:49:36,300 --> 00:49:38,320
the gradient for 4 will be positive

941
00:49:38,320 --> 00:49:39,900
and will encourage this behavior

942
00:49:39,900 --> 00:49:43,040
And then we'll discourage all the behavior for 1, 2, and 3

943
00:49:43,040 --> 00:49:45,040
Now, in some ways, this is good because it

944
00:49:45,040 --> 00:49:47,980
means that we are encouraging the good behavior

945
00:49:47,980 --> 00:49:49,440
But there are also some things that

946
00:49:49,440 --> 00:49:51,920
are a little bit dissatisfying about this

947
00:49:51,920 --> 00:49:55,630
The first is that the gradient for 1, 2, and 3

948
00:49:55,630 --> 00:49:57,830
is going to be identical for all of them

949
00:49:57,830 --> 00:50:01,190
There isn't any differentiation between them

950
00:50:01,190 --> 00:50:03,690
And some of these are actually better than others

951
00:50:03,690 --> 00:50:07,150
And this is, in some ways, a result of the reward function

952
00:50:07,150 --> 00:50:11,040
that we're using, which is more sparse than it could be

953
00:50:11,040 --> 00:50:12,790
And the other thing is that we'll actually

954
00:50:12,790 --> 00:50:17,110
see some techniques in Friday's lecture that would actually

955
00:50:17,110 --> 00:50:18,990
be able to leverage some of the good parts

956
00:50:18,990 --> 00:50:20,110
of these trajectories, because there

957
00:50:20,110 --> 00:50:21,860
is a good thing about folding the sleeves

958
00:50:21,860 --> 00:50:24,030
and it would be nice to be able to use that data

959
00:50:24,030 --> 00:50:25,610
to encourage the policy

960
00:50:25,610 --> 00:50:27,950
And right now, it will actually be discouraged

961
00:50:27,950 --> 00:50:30,990
and have negative gradients for this trajectory

962
00:50:30,990 --> 00:50:32,670
So in some ways, this is good

963
00:50:32,670 --> 00:50:34,790
So it will encourage folding, but the gradient

964
00:50:34,790 --> 00:50:38,230
is constant for trajectories 1, 2, and 3

965
00:50:38,230 --> 00:50:44,470
And on Friday, we'll see ways to make this a little bit better

966
00:50:44,470 --> 00:50:46,910
And one of the takeaways here is that policy gradient

967
00:50:46,910 --> 00:50:49,910
is still noisy and is still high variance

968
00:50:49,910 --> 00:50:51,390
And it really is going to work best

969
00:50:51,390 --> 00:50:54,960
if you have dense rewards rather than rewards that are sparse

970
00:50:54,960 --> 00:50:57,210
And it'll work best if you have large batches of data

971
00:50:57,210 --> 00:50:59,750
because if you didn't actually sample this fourth trajectory

972
00:50:59,750 --> 00:51:05,990
and you only sampled failures, then you would have 0 gradient

973
00:51:05,990 --> 00:51:09,110
And you wouldn't actually be able to improve

974
00:51:09,110 --> 00:51:13,250
So the question was, can we just improve the reward function

975
00:51:13,250 --> 00:51:13,750
Yes

976
00:51:13,750 --> 00:51:17,270
So if we had a more dense reward function

977
00:51:17,270 --> 00:51:20,030
that would definitely help in these cases

978
00:51:20,030 --> 00:51:26,430
There are actually scenarios still where we could do better

979
00:51:26,430 --> 00:51:28,770
Especially if you, for example, folded the sleeves

980
00:51:28,770 --> 00:51:30,350
you got positive reward for this

981
00:51:30,350 --> 00:51:32,470
and then dropped it on the ground or something

982
00:51:32,470 --> 00:51:34,510
after that, then that's still dissatisfying

983
00:51:34,510 --> 00:51:36,910
because we wouldn't use the first part successfully

984
00:51:36,910 --> 00:51:39,930
And we'll actually see how to get

985
00:51:39,930 --> 00:51:42,280
a good gradient for that first part on Friday, but yeah

986
00:51:42,280 --> 00:51:43,910
If you had a more dense reward function

987
00:51:43,910 --> 00:51:45,610
for this particular set of trajectories

988
00:51:45,610 --> 00:51:48,120
that would work a lot better

989
00:51:48,120 --> 00:51:50,330
Oh, so the question here is, when we do the baseline

990
00:51:50,330 --> 00:51:54,020
are we also using this trick to only use future rewards

991
00:51:54,020 --> 00:51:55,900
You can combine them

992
00:51:55,900 --> 00:51:57,980
You need to be a little bit--

993
00:51:57,980 --> 00:51:59,940
you need to think a little bit potentially more

994
00:51:59,940 --> 00:52:02,660
carefully about what the baseline will be

995
00:52:02,660 --> 00:52:06,110
because some of the rewards--

996
00:52:06,110 --> 00:52:08,240
you'll be summing over fewer things or more things

997
00:52:08,240 --> 00:52:10,180
depending on where you are in the trajectory

998
00:52:10,180 --> 00:52:12,580
but you can definitely combine them

999
00:52:12,580 --> 00:52:14,660
So the question was, if we're using average reward

1000
00:52:14,660 --> 00:52:17,440
as the baseline, do we need to keep updating the term

1001
00:52:17,440 --> 00:52:20,030
Do we need to use data from the policy

1002
00:52:20,030 --> 00:52:21,780
So yes, you'll want to keep on updating it

1003
00:52:21,780 --> 00:52:24,980
because as the policy gets better, above average

1004
00:52:24,980 --> 00:52:26,660
and below average will change

1005
00:52:26,660 --> 00:52:33,860
And you'll want to recompute it on your latest batch of data

1006
00:52:33,860 --> 00:52:35,340
Cool

1007
00:52:35,340 --> 00:52:37,680
So one more thing on this version of the algorithm

1008
00:52:37,680 --> 00:52:40,880
and then we'll move on to one last version

1009
00:52:40,880 --> 00:52:42,340
in terms of how to implement it, we

1010
00:52:42,340 --> 00:52:47,060
talked about-- we derived the gradient like this

1011
00:52:47,060 --> 00:52:50,900
And to actually implement this in practice

1012
00:52:50,900 --> 00:52:53,100
you could, in principle, actually

1013
00:52:53,100 --> 00:52:56,740
just run a backward pass for all of these different terms

1014
00:52:56,740 --> 00:52:58,720
But if you computed all of these individually

1015
00:52:58,720 --> 00:53:01,740
then you'd have to do n times t backward passes

1016
00:53:01,740 --> 00:53:04,940
and this would be rather inefficient

1017
00:53:04,940 --> 00:53:08,100
And it'd be nice if we could just actually do

1018
00:53:08,100 --> 00:53:10,300
automatic differentiation on the full objective

1019
00:53:10,300 --> 00:53:14,260
and only do one forward pass and one backward pass

1020
00:53:14,260 --> 00:53:15,880
And we can do that

1021
00:53:15,880 --> 00:53:17,900
So it's possible to implement essentially

1022
00:53:17,900 --> 00:53:20,100
a surrogate objective whose gradient

1023
00:53:20,100 --> 00:53:22,950
is the same as this objective

1024
00:53:22,950 --> 00:53:24,660
I'm calling it surrogate because it's not

1025
00:53:24,660 --> 00:53:27,820
the same as the very first objective of maximizing expected

1026
00:53:27,820 --> 00:53:30,030
rewards

1027
00:53:30,030 --> 00:53:31,860
But you can write down a surrogate objective

1028
00:53:31,860 --> 00:53:33,540
for this particular example

1029
00:53:33,540 --> 00:53:35,740
This is what the surrogate objective looks like

1030
00:53:35,740 --> 00:53:40,660
Note that I actually do have a typo on these equations, where

1031
00:53:40,660 --> 00:53:42,840
this second sum should be inside the first sum

1032
00:53:42,840 --> 00:53:43,960
That's maybe a small note

1033
00:53:43,960 --> 00:53:46,140
I'll see if I can fix that

1034
00:53:46,140 --> 00:53:48,750
But, yeah, you can implement a surrogate objective

1035
00:53:48,750 --> 00:53:52,130
and then use backprop and autodiff software

1036
00:53:52,130 --> 00:53:58,260
to just do one backward pass

1037
00:53:58,260 --> 00:54:00,130
And what this actually looks like in practice

1038
00:54:00,130 --> 00:54:02,340
is this would actually look like a cross-entropy loss

1039
00:54:02,340 --> 00:54:04,930
for a discrete action policy, or something like squared error

1040
00:54:04,930 --> 00:54:06,730
for our Gaussian policy

1041
00:54:06,730 --> 00:54:09,250
You could also use more sophisticated policies

1042
00:54:09,250 --> 00:54:12,990
like diffusion or something like that as well

1043
00:54:12,990 --> 00:54:13,490
OK

1044
00:54:13,490 --> 00:54:17,970
So summary so far, we showed how to estimate

1045
00:54:17,970 --> 00:54:21,810
the gradient of the RL objective using this log gradient trick

1046
00:54:21,810 --> 00:54:24,810
This corresponds to weighing the policy likelihood

1047
00:54:24,810 --> 00:54:26,410
by future rewards

1048
00:54:26,410 --> 00:54:30,330
And we found that subtracting a baseline, like average reward

1049
00:54:30,330 --> 00:54:34,290
will also further improve the gradient

1050
00:54:34,290 --> 00:54:37,490
Even with these two things, the gradient is still noisy

1051
00:54:37,490 --> 00:54:40,730
and so it's best with large batches and dense rewards

1052
00:54:40,730 --> 00:54:42,730
This is also our first online reinforcement

1053
00:54:42,730 --> 00:54:44,230
learning algorithm, where we iterate

1054
00:54:44,230 --> 00:54:47,490
between collecting a batch of data with our policy

1055
00:54:47,490 --> 00:54:49,030
and improve our policy

1056
00:54:49,030 --> 00:54:53,130
And it formalizes this trial-and-error learning

1057
00:54:53,130 --> 00:54:55,050
process

1058
00:54:55,050 --> 00:54:57,130
And the intuition for everything we've done so far

1059
00:54:57,130 --> 00:54:58,850
is do more of the high-reward stuff

1060
00:54:58,850 --> 00:55:01,810
and less of the low-reward stuff

1061
00:55:01,810 --> 00:55:02,310
OK

1062
00:55:02,310 --> 00:55:04,450
Now, the last thing we'll talk about today

1063
00:55:04,450 --> 00:55:08,490
is what else is troublesome about policy gradients

1064
00:55:08,490 --> 00:55:11,730
And one thing that we haven't really talked about that much

1065
00:55:11,730 --> 00:55:14,250
is the details of the sampling process

1066
00:55:14,250 --> 00:55:18,050
And this is what our gradient and our algorithm looks like

1067
00:55:18,050 --> 00:55:22,450
And note that in this gradient, this

1068
00:55:22,450 --> 00:55:25,410
assumes that we're sampling from pi-theta

1069
00:55:25,410 --> 00:55:30,650
And we change theta in step 3

1070
00:55:30,650 --> 00:55:34,970
And this means that every single time we take a single gradient

1071
00:55:34,970 --> 00:55:41,060
step, we need to recollect data to estimate our gradient

1072
00:55:41,060 --> 00:55:42,810
And this is kind of sad because usually we

1073
00:55:42,810 --> 00:55:45,210
do thousands and thousands of gradient steps

1074
00:55:45,210 --> 00:55:49,320
on neural networks

1075
00:55:49,320 --> 00:55:53,080
So yeah, that seems like a problem

1076
00:55:53,080 --> 00:55:55,660
And what this is called is it's called on-policy

1077
00:55:55,660 --> 00:55:59,040
It means that the gradient that we are measuring

1078
00:55:59,040 --> 00:56:03,120
requires data using our exact policy

1079
00:56:03,120 --> 00:56:04,800
And we can think about--

1080
00:56:04,800 --> 00:56:07,480
this is an attribute of an online RL algorithm, where

1081
00:56:07,480 --> 00:56:10,960
on-policy only uses data from the current policy

1082
00:56:10,960 --> 00:56:13,000
and off-policy means that you can reuse data

1083
00:56:13,000 --> 00:56:17,840
from other past policies

1084
00:56:17,840 --> 00:56:22,220
So can we derive an off-policy version of the policy gradient

1085
00:56:22,220 --> 00:56:24,480
And it's only going to be a little off-policy

1086
00:56:24,480 --> 00:56:25,600
And by that, I mean, we're just going

1087
00:56:25,600 --> 00:56:27,600
to try to be able to take more than one gradient

1088
00:56:27,600 --> 00:56:31,520
step on a single batch of data

1089
00:56:31,520 --> 00:56:34,780
And to do this, we can use importance sampling

1090
00:56:34,780 --> 00:56:37,240
And importance sampling is actually a really cool technique

1091
00:56:37,240 --> 00:56:41,640
that's useful outside of reinforcement learning as well

1092
00:56:41,640 --> 00:56:45,520
And it's thinking about this scenario

1093
00:56:45,520 --> 00:56:50,560
where you want to estimate a quantity, an expectation

1094
00:56:50,560 --> 00:56:52,900
without actually sampling from that distribution

1095
00:56:52,900 --> 00:57:00,760
So say you have some expectation using samples from p of x

1096
00:57:00,760 --> 00:57:04,560
and you want to estimate some function

1097
00:57:04,560 --> 00:57:07,440
f of x using those samples, but say

1098
00:57:07,440 --> 00:57:13,000
that you only have access to the ability to sample from q of x

1099
00:57:13,000 --> 00:57:15,800
So this is usually called your proposal distribution that's

1100
00:57:15,800 --> 00:57:19,960
able to propose samples to use

1101
00:57:19,960 --> 00:57:21,260
This is your old policy

1102
00:57:21,260 --> 00:57:23,100
We have samples from our old policy

1103
00:57:23,100 --> 00:57:26,640
but we want to be able to estimate a function of samples

1104
00:57:26,640 --> 00:57:30,840
of our new policy after we've taken a gradient step

1105
00:57:30,840 --> 00:57:34,080
So what you can do in this case if we write down

1106
00:57:34,080 --> 00:57:40,640
this expectation as an integral, it looks something like this

1107
00:57:40,640 --> 00:57:46,950
And as long as q of x has reasonable support

1108
00:57:46,950 --> 00:57:48,870
we can write this as--

1109
00:57:48,870 --> 00:58:00,350
we can add in this term that equals 1 into our equation

1110
00:58:00,350 --> 00:58:03,110
And then we can now actually write this as an expectation

1111
00:58:03,110 --> 00:58:05,350
with respect to q

1112
00:58:05,350 --> 00:58:13,030
So this is equal to an expectation with respect

1113
00:58:13,030 --> 00:58:24,950
to q of p of x divided by q of x times f of x

1114
00:58:24,950 --> 00:58:27,710
And so this means that we can actually estimate--

1115
00:58:27,710 --> 00:58:33,310
we can get this expectation by sampling from q

1116
00:58:33,310 --> 00:58:36,770
and then weighting the quantity by p/q

1117
00:58:36,770 --> 00:58:40,150
[INAUDIBLE] So that's pretty cool

1118
00:58:40,150 --> 00:58:42,830
This means that we can use principal samples

1119
00:58:42,830 --> 00:58:47,710
from our old policy q, weight them

1120
00:58:47,710 --> 00:58:50,870
by the probability of the sample under our new policy divided

1121
00:58:50,870 --> 00:58:57,070
by the probability under the old policy times the quantity

1122
00:58:57,070 --> 00:59:00,230
So we showed for this simple case

1123
00:59:00,230 --> 00:59:04,870
of p of x and q of x in terms of actually applying this

1124
00:59:04,870 --> 00:59:08,670
to our policy

1125
00:59:08,670 --> 00:59:12,930
We can take our expected rewards with regard to p of theta

1126
00:59:12,930 --> 00:59:15,190
if we instead have some previous policy that we've

1127
00:59:15,190 --> 00:59:16,550
collected samples from

1128
00:59:16,550 --> 00:59:18,950
We can then measure our objective

1129
00:59:18,950 --> 00:59:21,670
in terms of the samples from that policy, where

1130
00:59:21,670 --> 00:59:26,150
we weight the reward by the new policy divided by the old policy

1131
00:59:26,150 --> 00:59:27,950
probability

1132
00:59:27,950 --> 00:59:32,510
Now, note that this requires your proposal distribution

1133
00:59:32,510 --> 00:59:38,410
to have nonzero support in scenarios

1134
00:59:38,410 --> 00:59:41,460
where your new policy has probability

1135
00:59:41,460 --> 00:59:44,060
This is actually very important for it to work if q of x is 0

1136
00:59:44,060 --> 00:59:47,580
This is not poorly defined

1137
00:59:47,580 --> 00:59:49,700
But that's really the only catch here

1138
00:59:49,700 --> 00:59:51,820
And in general, this importance sampling technique

1139
00:59:51,820 --> 00:59:54,340
is really going to work best when p and q--

1140
00:59:54,340 --> 00:59:56,220
or when your old policy and your new policy--

1141
00:59:56,220 --> 01:00:00,980
are very similar to each other

1142
01:00:00,980 --> 01:00:02,480
Now there's some details--

1143
01:00:02,480 --> 01:00:04,160
we've shown this in terms of p of tau

1144
01:00:04,160 --> 01:00:07,980
now how do we actually do this in terms of pi of a given S

1145
01:00:07,980 --> 01:00:10,780
That actually gets a little bit trickier

1146
01:00:10,780 --> 01:00:15,780
We can write down p of tau and this ratio

1147
01:00:15,780 --> 01:00:19,580
as the product of these different probabilities

1148
01:00:19,580 --> 01:00:23,180
Unfortunately, some of the probabilities cancel out

1149
01:00:23,180 --> 01:00:29,550
But when we then try to put these probabilities

1150
01:00:29,550 --> 01:00:32,220
into our update, we're going to get something

1151
01:00:32,220 --> 01:00:37,140
like this, where we then have this product

1152
01:00:37,140 --> 01:00:41,140
of these probability ratios

1153
01:00:41,140 --> 01:00:43,420
And now, unfortunately, this product

1154
01:00:43,420 --> 01:00:46,460
can actually become very small or very large

1155
01:00:46,460 --> 01:00:49,820
if the length of your trajectory is very large

1156
01:00:49,820 --> 01:00:51,940
because you're going to be multiplying

1157
01:00:51,940 --> 01:00:53,460
many, many numbers together

1158
01:00:53,460 --> 01:00:55,860
If you have 100 numbers that you're multiplying together

1159
01:00:55,860 --> 01:00:58,800
if they're above 1, that might start to get very large

1160
01:00:58,800 --> 01:01:02,140
If they're below 1, that might get to 0 very quickly

1161
01:01:02,140 --> 01:01:06,100
And so in practice, we often will consider the expectations

1162
01:01:06,100 --> 01:01:09,260
over time steps instead of trajectories

1163
01:01:09,260 --> 01:01:12,300
And that also has a little bit of a hitch, which

1164
01:01:12,300 --> 01:01:16,500
is that this needs to be over state action pairs

1165
01:01:16,500 --> 01:01:19,660
rather than actions given states

1166
01:01:19,660 --> 01:01:22,420
But often in practice, we can approximate

1167
01:01:22,420 --> 01:01:24,440
this ratio as equal to 1

1168
01:01:24,440 --> 01:01:27,720
This is incorrect, but it's the best that we can do

1169
01:01:27,720 --> 01:01:29,420
And then we can get some final form

1170
01:01:29,420 --> 01:01:37,970
where we get this ratio of new policy over old policy

1171
01:01:37,970 --> 01:01:40,330
Cool

1172
01:01:40,330 --> 01:01:41,900
The full algorithm looks the same

1173
01:01:41,900 --> 01:01:43,570
except now we can take multiple gradient

1174
01:01:43,570 --> 01:01:45,410
steps on the same batch

1175
01:01:45,410 --> 01:01:46,110
This is good

1176
01:01:46,110 --> 01:01:53,130
And then we have this new form for our gradient

1177
01:01:53,130 --> 01:01:54,600
OK

1178
01:01:54,600 --> 01:01:57,710
We'll talk a little bit about some tricks

1179
01:01:57,710 --> 01:02:01,170
We don't have time to cover KL constraints today

1180
01:02:01,170 --> 01:02:03,930
but to review things, we talked about online RL

1181
01:02:03,930 --> 01:02:05,590
It's an on-policy algorithm

1182
01:02:05,590 --> 01:02:07,830
We talked about baselines for reducing variance

1183
01:02:07,830 --> 01:02:10,550
And we also just derived this off-policy policy gradient

1184
01:02:10,550 --> 01:02:13,090
which uses importance sampling and allows

1185
01:02:13,090 --> 01:02:14,720
us to apply multiple gradient steps

1186
01:02:14,720 --> 01:02:16,970
The intuition is to do more high-reward stuff and less

1187
01:02:16,970 --> 01:02:18,110
low-reward stuff

1188
01:02:18,110 --> 01:02:20,250
And the gradient is still very noisy and best

1189
01:02:20,250 --> 01:02:24,610
with large batches and dense rewards

1190
01:02:24,610 --> 01:02:25,350
Cool

1191
01:02:25,350 --> 01:02:26,350
And that's it for today

1192
01:02:26,350 --> 01:02:28,640
Next time, we'll talk about actor-critic methods, which

1193
01:02:28,640 --> 01:02:30,910
we'll build closely on what we talked about today

1194
01:02:30,910 --> 01:02:32,840
including PPO

